    "note": "14 of the 18 blind claim statements are bridged or formally refuted. These statements are as follows. For Σ_3: the subgroup is unique and normal. For Σ_4: there are 1 or 4 subgroups, all conjugate, the image of ⟨(012)⟩ is not normal, and there are exactly 4. Also, 9 | n! (n > 5). All order-3 subgroups of Σ_3, Σ_4 and Σ_5 are conjugate, because they are 3-Sylow. They are not all conjugate for every n > 5 (module 1052). Also, |⟨(123)⟩| = 3. The corrected conjugacy statement is ⟨(123)⟩ ~ ⟨(456)⟩ and ⟨(123)⟩ ≁ ⟨(123)(456)⟩. Finally, there are formal refutations of three literal claims. The first is n(n-1)(n-2)/6 at n = 6: 20 ≢ 1 mod 3, but the number of subgroups of order p is ≡ 1 mod p (module 1051). The second is ⟨(123)⟩ not conjugate to ⟨(456)⟩. The third is \"one order-9 subgroup per 3-subset\": there are 20 subsets, but |Syl_3(Σ_6)| ≡ 1 mod 3. Gaps: blind_sigman_order3_count_corrected is bridged only for n = 3, 4. Modules 1053-1054 prove the numbers 10 (n = 5) and 40 (n = 6) (sigma5_order_three_subgroups_card, sigma6_order_three_subgroups_card). But the bridge to the blind statement is not included. Also open: blind_sigma6_order9_count (10 subgroups of order 9) and blind_sigma6_order9_per_triple_corrected (bijection with the splittings). The third open statement is blind_sigma6_order9_join (an order-9 subgroup that contains ⟨(123)⟩ and ⟨(456)⟩). They need the subgroups of order 9 of Σ_6. An enumeration over its 720 elements needs too much memory in the pinned Narya."
